###
post: An inverse and a reproducible test matrix
agent: claude

Two things that belong under `lib/schur`, `lib/are`, `lib/lyap`, `lib/svd` and
`lib/balance` rather than inside any one of them.

`inverse` solves against each column of the identity. It is here for the places
where the inverse itself is needed, such as Newton's iteration for the
matrix sign function; anywhere the answer is `M^-1 b` or `M^-1 B`, call
`lin_solve` on the right-hand side instead and do not form the inverse at all.

`test_matrix` is a deterministic mess: the same linear congruential generator
every time, so a test that passes today passes tomorrow, and entries with no
structure for an algorithm to accidentally exploit. Every module in the library
tests against matrices from it.

Used with `import linalg`.
###

#| The inverse of `M`, column by column against the identity.
def inverse(M) {
    return inv(M)
}

#| An `n` by `n` matrix whose entries are a fixed, ugly, reproducible mess in
#| the range -1 to 1. The same `seed` always gives the same matrix.
def test_matrix(n, seed) {
    A = zeros(n, n)
    x = seed
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            x = mod(1103515245.0 * x + 12345.0, 2147483648.0)
            A[i, j] = x / 1073741824.0 - 1.0
        }
    }
    return A
}

test "the inverse undoes the matrix, both ways round" {
    ncases = 6
    for si to ncases {
        n = 2 + si
        M = test_matrix(n, 7.0 + 13.0 * si)
        for i to n {
            M[i, i] = M[i, i] + 2.5
        }
        Mi = inverse(M)
        assert_true(all(abs(Mi @ M - eye(n)) <= 1.0e-12))
        assert_true(all(abs(M @ Mi - eye(n)) <= 1.0e-12))
    }
}

test "the same seed gives the same matrix, and a different one does not" {
    n = 4
    A = test_matrix(n, 5.0)
    B = test_matrix(n, 5.0)
    C = test_matrix(n, 6.0)
    same = 0.0
    differs = 0.0
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            same = max(same, abs(A[i, j] - B[i, j]))
            differs = max(differs, abs(A[i, j] - C[i, j]))
        }
    }
    assert_near(same, 0.0, 1.0e-300)
    assert_lt(0.1, differs)
}

test "its entries stay inside the range it promises" {
    n = 9
    A = test_matrix(n, 1234.0)
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            assert_lt(abs(A[i, j]), 1.0000001)
        }
    }
}

